{
  "description": "The `stop` half of the same rule (spec/execution-model.md:657-660): `return`, `output`, `op`, and `stop` inside a handler block are all outside any procedure, so this raises `ol-stop-outside-proc`, whose params are empty by registry (spec/error-model.md:119). Written as a separate fixture from its `return` sibling because execution stops at the first error, so one program cannot assert both codes. Added at the logic/spec reviewer's request during the review gate, which measured that no executing handler fixture in the corpus used a `stop` statement at all. Why the after-return shape needs only these two fixtures while the on-stack shape needs one per handler KIND: measured, the per-site conversion and the top-level one at `executeMainLine` are mutually redundant for an after-return firing — remove either alone and the other still produces the same diagnostic — so an after-return fixture cannot discriminate the conversion under any single-site mutation. What closes the remaining kind/signal cells is that both shapes route through the same `invokeXHandler`: a handler kind that discards an escaping `return`/`stop` entirely — the strongest per-kind defect, breaking both shapes at once — is caught by that kind's on-stack sibling in every case, measured for all four kinds.",
